StatefulWidget lifecycle
createState()
     ↓
initState()
     ↓
didChangeDependencies()
     ↓
build()
     ↓
setState() → build() again
     ↓
didUpdateWidget()
     ↓
deactivate()
     ↓
dispose()
1. createState()

Creates the State object for the StatefulWidget.

2. initState()

Called once when the State object is inserted into the tree.

Used for initialization, like:

@override
void initState() {
  super.initState();
  // initialize something
}
3. didChangeDependencies()

Called when an inherited dependency used by the widget changes.

Example: Theme, MediaQuery, etc.

4. build()

Builds/returns the UI.

It can run many times, especially after setState().

5. didUpdateWidget()

Called when the parent rebuilds and provides a new Widget configuration of the same type.

6. deactivate()

Called when the State is temporarily removed from the tree.

7. dispose()

Called when the State is permanently removed.

Used for cleanup:

controller.dispose();
Your answer correction

You said:

init → build → didChangeDependencies → dispose → detach

You're remembering the right concepts. Just remember:

deactivate(), not detach().

And don't worry about memorizing every lifecycle method perfectly yet. For interviews, initState → build → didChangeDependencies → didUpdateWidget → deactivate → dispose is the useful sequence.